WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.219
Storage duration and object lifetime are central to memory-safe C.

2
00:00:04.219 --> 00:00:12.082
An object is safe to access only while its lifetime is active and only through operations that respect its type and bounds.

3
00:00:12.082 --> 00:00:20.520
A pointer may still contain an address after an object has gone away, but the program no longer has a valid object at that lifetime.

4
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.419
Automatic objects usually live for a block or function scope.

5
00:00:24.419 --> 00:00:34.135
Local variables, local arrays, and local structures are convenient for temporary work, but their lifetime ends when execution leaves the relevant scope.

6
00:00:34.135 --> 00:00:43.149
Returning a pointer to such an object, or storing that address for later use, creates a pointer to storage whose intended lifetime has ended.

7
00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:46.473
Static objects live for the duration of the program.

8
00:00:46.473 --> 00:00:54.399
They can be appropriate for constants, shared state, or long-lived tables, but long lifetime is not the same as safe access.

9
00:00:54.399 --> 00:01:02.581
Static storage can still create data races, hidden dependencies, stale state, or confusing ownership if the contract is unclear.

10
00:01:02.581 --> 00:01:06.481
Dynamically allocated objects live until explicitly released.

11
00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:13.640
That flexibility is useful when a result must outlive a function call or when capacity is determined at runtime.

12
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:22.845
It also creates responsibility: someone must own the allocation, update metadata consistently, and release it exactly according to the contract.

13
00:01:22.845 --> 00:01:28.918
Dangling pointers occur when code keeps using an address after the object's lifetime has ended.

14
00:01:28.918 --> 00:01:41.575
This can happen after a local object goes out of scope, after dynamic storage is released, after a buffer is reallocated, or after ownership is transferred but the old owner keeps using the pointer.

15
00:01:41.575 --> 00:01:46.689
Function contracts and cleanup logic should make lifetime and ownership visible.

16
00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:58.451
A caller should know whether it owns returned storage, whether it may retain a borrowed pointer, whether the callee stores a pointer, and which cleanup path is responsible for release.

17
00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:02.286
Clear lifetime rules reduce whole classes of memory defects.
